        "# My PageRank Project: Understanding how Google ranks pages with Linear Algebra\n",
        "\n",
        "## 1. What is this project about?\n",
        "When we search for something on Google, how does it know which page is the most important? In this project, I want to explore the PageRank algorithm. It was made by Larry Page and Sergey Brin in 1998.\n",
        "\n",
        "Instead of just counting words on a page, PageRank looks at links. If a website has many links from other websites, it is probably important. But also, if a very important website (like Wikipedia) links to you, your page gets more points than a link from a small personal blog.\n",
        "\n",
        "## 2. The Math behind it (Simplified)\n",
        "We can think of the internet as a big graph where pages are dots (nodes) and links are arrows (edges).\n",
        "\n",
        "Let's say we have a web page A. The score of page A depends on the scores of all pages linking to it:\n",
        "\n",
        "PR(A) = PR(B)/L(B) + PR(C)/L(C)\n",
        "\n",
        "Where L(B) is how many total links page B has. We divide by L(B) because if page B links to 100 pages, its \"vote\" is split into 100 small pieces.\n",
        "\n",
        "### The Problem: Dead Ends and Traps\n",
        "When coding this, I found out two big problems:\n",
        "1. **Dead ends (Dangling nodes):** A page with no links out. The score goes there and disappears.\n",
        "2. **Spider traps:** Pages that only link to each other. The score gets stuck in a loop.\n",
        "\n",
        "To fix this, we use a **Damping Factor (d)**, usually 0.85. It means that 85% of the time, a user clicks on real links on the page. 15% of the time, they get bored and just type a completely random website URL in the browser. This keeps the math working!"
      ],
      "metadata": {
        "id": "A6QVCQA7UXz3"
      }
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": [
        "import numpy as np\n",
        "\n",
        "# Step 1: Let's create a simple network of 4 web pages\n",
        "# Page 0 links to page 1 and 2\n",
        "# Page 1 links to page 3\n",
        "# Page 2 links to page 0 and 3\n",
        "# Page 3 links only to page 0\n",
        "\n",
        "H = np.array([\n",
        "    [0,   0,   0.5, 1], # Links going to Page 0\n",
        "    [0.5, 0,   0,   0], # Links going to Page 1\n",
        "    [0.5, 0,   0,   0], # Links going to Page 2\n",
        "    [0,   1,   0.5, 0]  # Links going to Page 3\n",
        "])\n",
        "\n",
        "print(\"Our initial link matrix H:\")\n",
        "print(H)\n",
        "\n",
        "# Step 2: # Step 2: Configuration parameters\n",
        "d = 0.85  # Google damping factor\n",
        "n = 4    # Number of pages\n",
        "epsilon = 0.001 # Convergence thre\n",
        "\n",
        "# Step 3: Base Google Matrix formula\n",
        "ones_matrix = np.ones((n, n))\n",
        "G = d * H + ((1 - d) / n) * ones_matrix\n",
        "\n",
        "print(\"\\nThe Google Matrix G (with random surfer jump):\")\n",
        "print(G)\n",
        "\n",
        "# Step 4: Start with equal ranks for everyone\n",
        "r = np.array([0.25, 0.25, 0.25, 0.25])\n",
        "\n",
        "# Step 5: Power Iteration loop\n",
        "iterations = 0\n",
        "for i in range(100):\n",
        "    next_r = np.dot(G, r)\n",
        "    iterations += 1\n",
        "\n",
        "    diff = np.sum(np.abs(next_r - r))\n",
        "    if diff < epsilon:\n",
        "        r = next_r\n",
        "        break\n",
        "\n",
        "    r = next_r\n",
        "\n",
        "print(f\"\\nAlgorithm converged after {iterations} iterations.\")\n",
        "print(\"Final PageRank scores:\")\n",
        "for page, score in enumerate(r):\n",
        "    print(f\"Page {page}: {score:.4f}\")\n"
      ],
